{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "noise": "L"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 120, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding the input-output residual where none existed gives far larger gains than swapping a conv/pool on edge 3 for skip (e.g. #11, #19, #20 vs #12-14)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the identity shortcut is generally more damaging than removing a conv shortcut on edge 3 (#61, #68 vs #62)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Without an input-output residual, a skip from node 1 restores a short gradient path (#95-96 gain +4 to +9 on all datasets vs about neutral elsewhere)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 4 is absent, dropping edge 5 leaves node 3 fed only by edge 3, removing all learned computation (#72-73 vs #71)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 1 absent, removing edge 0 disconnects nodes 1 and 2 from the input, collapsing the cell (#29-30 large drops)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing a parameter-free pool on the first edge with a conv adds much-needed capacity (#83, #87 vs #81-82, #84)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Swapping pooling for a conv on edge 1 gains more than replacing none or a 1x1 conv (#54, #59 vs #51, #55)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 5 is absent, edge 4 is the main learned input to the output node, so adding a conv there helps a lot (#40)."}, {"edit_edge": 2, 